course: dictionary
lesson: 9731
title: Sunk: under water, set low, or in trouble
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Sunk» is a form of the verb sink. The past tense is sank, and sunk is the form we use with has, have or had: «The ship had sunk before the crew got off.» Learn the three forms (sink, sank, sunk) and you can spot this word anywhere.
explain: Context gives you clues. Near water, ships or the sea bed, sunk means gone under the surface. Near money and spending, it means something is already lost. «I can't stop now, I've already spent twenty quid.» The money is gone, so spending more will not bring it back.
explain: Always check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. «If we miss the bus, we're sunk» means we are in deep trouble with no way out. Does that fit? Yes, so you have understood the word.
fact: Sunk is a form of the verb sink, which means to go down under water or below a surface.
fact: «The Titanic sank in 1912 and lies sunk on the sea bed.»
fact: Sunk can also mean set lower, as in «sunk into the sofa».
fact: Informal: «If you are sunk, you are in deep trouble with no way out.»
fact: The sunk cost fallacy means spending more because you have already spent. Money spent is gone whatever you do next.
exercise: The ship had ___ to the bottom of the sea. | sinks | sank | *sunk | sinking
exercise: «The sunk cost fallacy is spending more because you have already spent.» What does this warn you about? | Spending is always a good idea | Money in the bank is always safe | *Money already spent is gone, whatever you do next | Ships always float
exercise: Ben says, «If we miss the bus, we're sunk.» What does sunk mean here? | Set lower, like in a sofa | Under water on the sea bed | *In deep trouble with no way out | Very tired after a long walk
test: The stone was heavy, so it ___ to the bottom of the pond. | sinks | *sank | sunk | sinking
test: «The stone had ___ into the mud.» Which word fits? | *sunk | sinks | sank | sinking
exam: What is the best way to avoid the sunk cost trap? | Hide the bill and hope it goes away | *Decide by what will happen next, not by what is already gone | Keep spending, because you have already spent so much | Spend more to get your money back
